      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=3{e}^{x}-{x}^{2}+5x-2$ in the interval $[-1,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.6.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(3{e}^{x}-{x}^{2}+5x-2)^{′}=3{e}^{x}-2x+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{x}_{n}^{2}+5{x}_{n}-2}{3{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2{x}_{n}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2{x}_{n}+5)-(3{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{x}_{n}^{2}+5{x}_{n}-2)}{3{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2{x}_{n}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{x}_{n}^{2}-3{e}^{{x}_{n}}+2}{3{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2{x}_{n}+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}-{x}_{0}^{2}-3{e}^{{x}_{0}}+2}{3{e}^{{x}_{0}}-2{x}_{0}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 0.6{e}^{0.6}-(0.6)^{2}-3{e}^{0.6}+2}{3{e}^{0.6}-2\\cdot 0.6+5} \\\\  & =-0.058\\,981….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{1}^{2}-3{e}^{{x}_{1}}+2}{3{e}^{{x}_{1}}-2{x}_{1}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot (-0.058\\,981…){e}^{-0.058\\,981…}-(-0.058\\,981…)^{2}-3{e}^{-0.058\\,981…}+2}{3{e}^{-0.058\\,981…}-2\\cdot (-0.058\\,981…)+5} \\\\  & =-0.125\\,654….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈-0.059$ and ${x}_{2}≈-0.126,$ rounded to three decimal places.",
